{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact 133cd39c5e955f2fc8edeb7b4b28544848cc5dff for #19205, with selected implementation Report #19241 (0d749c1a) and the eleven exact changed-path artifacts named here. I reviewed the clean detached checkout, compared the complete diff with delivered b2ed9266, verified the artifact manifest against that diff, and inspected the governing request and live implementation promise. This verdict covers source delivery; it does not activate a resident or change Workroom authority.\n\nArchitecture: the changed layers are CLI transport, the semantic-free kernel verification entry point, and application import/configuration coordination. [CLI attachment](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/blob/133cd39c5e955f2fc8edeb7b4b28544848cc5dff/cmd/gs/main.go#L3003) places remote observations in a separate namespace and fetches the selected genesis exactly. [AttachSequence](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/blob/133cd39c5e955f2fc8edeb7b4b28544848cc5dff/internal/app/attach.go) verifies immutable history, checks continuation of both the pre-fetch canonical ref and the saved frontier, then compares the ref while holding the existing configuration update lock. VerifyAt reuses the kernel scanner. The [architecture page](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/blob/133cd39c5e955f2fc8edeb7b4b28544848cc5dff/docs/reference/architecture.md#L563), candidate artifact ffaa81cf, documents these changes and both partial-state boundaries. Kernel signatures/history, apphost custody and Workroom authority retain their contracts.\n\nSecurity: the checked real-Git receiving hook independently demonstrated the legacy mapping losing a concurrent signed append and the corrected mapping preserving it during a successful push. I ran migration controls for both legacy rules, separately and together, preserving custom mappings/configuration; initial/repeated attachment; remote deletion despite retained tracking; rollback, valid signed sibling and unsigned-history refusal; checkpoint-ahead and writable-custody refusal; and concurrent import CAS failure. Prior canonical state and memory remain intact on those refusals. The first-import losing CAS leaves only the documented read-only identity, no frontier or signing custody, and preserves the winning ref. Real post-CAS permission failures preserve old memory and a newer append, explicitly report partial failure, and recover by verified retry or audit. There is no compensating rewind, witness reset, forced canonical import or second authority path. I found no unresolved security defect in this change.\n\nSimplification: one existing scanner, one shared frontier-continuation check, the existing config lock and Git CAS implement the required behavior. The small deterministic test seams expose the relevant boundaries without adding a recovery subsystem. No additional abstraction or task state is needed, and no further source simplification is required for these conditions.\n\nDocumentation staleness was examined, not waived. All eleven final artifacts are live; none describes a superseded world. I independently inspected all nine ordinary-stale documentation bases. Six exact source paths are unchanged at this head: service preview, address handling, connector CLI, Git preview, Preview and Notes. The wider gitstore basis differs only by the already-described preview addition; the kernel basis differs only by the reviewed VerifyAt addition. The residentclient difference is the previously delivered PID/forward-compatible identity probe; ordinary response decoding remains strict. The four updated documents match the reviewed behavior and retain these dependencies. Historical causes include the JSONata spike, testgit and earlier documentation/connector succession; they do not change this immutable candidate. Retired inherited references have been replaced by live implementation bases rather than revived.\n\nValidation: independent focused -race attachment/publication controls passed, go vet ./... passed, and diff --check passed. My full go test -race ./... run at this exact head exited 1: 33 packages passed, including cmd/gs, app, kernel, apphost, residentclient and docset. Two existing test fixtures failed: the cancellation omission oracle at 5.02s, already assigned in #19212, and TestWhoamiRetriesOneConcurrentFrontierMove at 4.28s, now assigned separately in request a8475101. One isolated same-head whoami comparison passed in 1.28s; it does not erase the full-suite failure. That unchanged fixture performs signing/snapshot work inside the production two-second resident budget, so load-sensitive test behavior is supported, though this run did not instrument the exact elapsed sub-step. The separate request must establish and correct that cause without weakening the retry or timeout conditions. Full and isolated logs are preserved with that request.\n\nI independently checked [CI 34027293040](https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/actions/runs/34027293040) and Security 34027293027 successful on exact133cd39c5. The implementation evidence distinguishes its earlier23961 tests, final fresh CI and additional local full run. Its local docset refusal correctly retained a newer shared checkpoint; I read the attached failure and the separate real-record snapshot validation at frontier19241, which passed with read-only custody and a removed fixture. My independent docset run also passed. These disclosed test-quality issues remain tracked and do not establish a publication regression. Approval does not claim that either failed local full run was green.\n\nCodex should ratify this exact verdict, use the sealed merge path, push source/sequence/receipt and remove the owned worktree/branch. Keep #19212 and the separate whoami correction live through their own reviewed delivery.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0d749c1a07e2ae3daa83d4d1a5473fe422bce3bf","head":"133cd39c5e955f2fc8edeb7b4b28544848cc5dff",